.TH "HDW4S" "8" "September 2026" "hdw4s" "hdw4s"
.SH "NAME"
\fBhdw4s\fR \- headless GNOME desktop streamed to a web browser
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
\fBhdw4s\fR \fBlist\fR
.br
\fBhdw4s\fR \fBshow\fR \fIinstance\fR
.br
\fBhdw4s\fR \fBenable\fR \fIinstance\fR
.br
\fBhdw4s\fR \fBdisable\fR \fIinstance\fR
.br
\fBhdw4s\fR \fBrelease\fR \fIinstance\fR
.br
\fBhdw4s\fR \fBtransport\fR \fIinstance\fR \fBtcp\fR|\fBunix\fR
.br
\fBhdw4s\fR \fBauth\fR \fIinstance\fR
.br
\fBhdw4s\fR \fBproxy\fR \fIinstance\fR
.br
\fBhdw4s\fR \fBset\fR [\fIinstance\fR] \fIKEY\fR=\fIVALUE\fR\|\.\|\.\|\.
.br
\fBhdw4s\fR \fBunset\fR [\fIinstance\fR] \fIKEY\fR
.br
\fBhdw4s\fR \fBseed\fR \fIinstance\fR [\fB\-\-only\fR \fIpath\fR]\|\.\|\.\|\.
.br
\fBhdw4s\fR \fBkeyring\fR \fIinstance\fR
.br
\fBhdw4s\fR \fBfirewall\fR \fB\-\-apply\fR|\fB\-\-check\fR|\fB\-\-print\fR
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
\fBhdw4s\fR gives an existing account on an existing machine a full GNOME desktop that is reachable from a web browser\. It needs no graphics card and no container: the point is to reach the machine and the account that are already there\.
.P
Each session is a systemd unit that runs, as that user, an X server on the \fBdummy\fR driver, a GNOME session, and the Selkies streaming server\. The X server never touches a virtual terminal, a DRM device or an input device, which is what lets an ordinary account start one from a service with no seat and no login\.
.P
Sessions are expected to sit behind a reverse proxy that terminates TLS and identifies the user\. The streaming server itself runs with authentication switched off, so that a user who is already known to the proxy lands on their desktop without a second login\. See \fBREVERSE PROXY AND SECURITY\fR\.
.SH "COMMANDS"
.TP
\fBlist\fR
Print every configured session with its user, port, unit state, whether anything is listening on its port, and its current display\. This is the quickest way to see whether reality matches the configuration\.
.TP
\fBshow\fR \fIinstance\fR
Print one session's settings and, for each of them, which file it came from\.
.TP
\fBenable\fR \fIinstance\fR
Allocate a slot for a session, start it, and start it again at boot\.
.TP
\fBdisable\fR \fIinstance\fR
Stop a session and stop starting it at boot\. The slot stays reserved, so the port does not change if the session is enabled again\.
.TP
\fBrelease\fR \fIinstance\fR
Stop a session and give up its slot, so another session may take the port\. The session's profile directory is left in place\.
.TP
\fBtransport\fR \fIinstance\fR \fBtcp\fR|\fBunix\fR
Choose how the reverse proxy reaches this session\. See \fBTRANSPORTS\fR\.
.TP
\fBauth\fR \fIinstance\fR
Require the proxy to present a secret as well as an acceptable address\. Generated here, sealed to this machine, and never shown to the user, so there is still no login screen\.
.TP
\fBproxy\fR \fIinstance\fR
Print an nginx configuration for this session, matching whichever transport and authentication it is set up for\.
.TP
\fBset\fR [\fIinstance\fR] \fIKEY\fR=\fIVALUE\fR\|\.\|\.\|\.
Change a setting without opening an editor\. With an instance, writes to that session's file; without one, to the defaults\. The edit is deliberately conservative: an existing assignment is changed where it stands, keeping its indentation and any note written after it on the same line; otherwise the documented default is uncommented in place, so the explanation above it still applies; otherwise the setting is appended\. Nothing else in the file is touched, and anything not offered here can still be edited by hand\.
.TP
\fBunset\fR [\fIinstance\fR] \fIKEY\fR
Comment a setting out so the default applies again\. The line is commented rather than deleted, so the value that was there stays visible\.
.TP
\fBseed\fR \fIinstance\fR [\fB\-\-only\fR \fIpath\fR]\|\.\|\.\|\.
Copy an account's existing desktop settings from its home directory into an isolated session's profile, once\. Only ever in that direction, only when asked, and only into a profile that is still empty \-\- a profile with anything in it is one somebody has used, and there is no way to tell which of two copies of a setting is the newer\. Re\-running is refused rather than overwriting; remove the profile first if starting over is really meant\.
.IP
This is a snapshot and not a link\. The two diverge from that moment on\.
.IP
\fB\-\-only\fR limits the copy to the named paths, relative to the home directory, and may be given more than once\. An account that has been in use for years accumulates settings for programs that will never run in this session; copying all of it forward only carries the accumulation into a second place\. Naming what matters \-\- the settings database, the browser profile, the handful of applications actually used \-\- is usually a great deal less than what is there\. Paths that do not exist are reported and skipped, and the exclusions above still apply inside the paths that do\.
.IP "" 4
.nf
hdw4s seed alice \-\-only \.config/dconf \-\-only \.config/google\-chrome
.fi
.IP "" 0
.IP
Caches are skipped, including the ones browsers keep inside their profile directories rather than under \fB~/\.cache\fR; they are regenerable and are usually almost all of the size\. So are downloaded models and component data: a current Chrome fetches an on\-device language model of nearly three gigabytes, per profile, which dwarfs everything worth carrying across\. Saved passwords are skipped too, since the session has its own keyring with its own password\.
.IP
The session must be stopped, or the copy captures half\-written state\.
.TP
\fBkeyring\fR \fIinstance\fR
Give a session its own keyring with a generated password, sealed to this machine with systemd\-creds and handed to the session at start time, so it is unlocked without anyone typing anything\. The session's keyring is separate from the account's login keyring: passwords saved in one are not visible in the other\. Requires root and a restart of the session\.
.TP
\fBfirewall \-\-apply\fR
Rewrite the package's nftables table and the kernel's reserved\-port list from the current configuration\. Run this after changing \fBHDW4S_PROXIES\fR, \fBHDW4S_BASE_PORT\fR or \fBHDW4S_BLOCK_SIZE\fR\.
.TP
\fBfirewall \-\-check\fR
Report whether the table is loaded and whether every session listens on a port the table actually covers\. Exits non\-zero if not\.
.TP
\fBfirewall \-\-restore\fR
Put the table back if it has gone missing, and do nothing if it has not\. Run periodically by \fBhdw4s\-firewall\.timer\fR; see \fBREVERSE PROXY AND SECURITY\fR\.
.TP
\fBfirewall \-\-print\fR
Write the ruleset to standard output without loading it\.
.SH "INSTANCES"
An instance is a user name, optionally followed by a colon and a session number:
.IP "" 4
.nf
hdw4s enable alice
hdw4s enable alice:2
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
The second form gives one account a second concurrent desktop on the same machine\. A colon is used because systemd leaves it untouched when it escapes an instance name, and because it cannot occur in a user name, so splitting on it can never cut a name in half\.
.SH "CONFIGURATION"
Three files are read in order, each overriding the last:
.IP "" 4
.nf
/etc/hdw4s/hdw4s\.conf         defaults for every session
/etc/hdw4s/<instance>\.conf    settings for one session
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
\fBhdw4s show\fR \fIinstance\fR prints the result and where each value came from\.
.TP
\fBHDW4S_PROXIES\fR
Addresses allowed to reach a session, separated by spaces; IPv4 and IPv6 addresses and prefixes are both accepted\. Empty means this machine only\.
.TP
\fBHDW4S_ISOLATION\fR
\fBnone\fR or \fBprofile\fR\. See \fBSHARED HOME DIRECTORIES\fR\. Defaults to \fBnone\fR\.
.TP
\fBHDW4S_PROFILE_DIR\fR
Where an isolated session keeps its private profile\. Defaults to \fB/var/lib/hdw4s\fR\.
.TP
\fBHDW4S_BASE_PORT\fR, \fBHDW4S_BLOCK_SIZE\fR
The block of TCP ports sessions are allocated from\. Defaults to 7300 and 64\.
.TP
\fBHDW4S_ADDR\fR
The address the streaming server binds\. Defaults to \fB0\.0\.0\.0\fR, because the reverse proxy is normally on another machine\. What limits who may connect is the firewall, not this\.
.TP
\fBHDW4S_PORT\fR
Overrides the allocated port for one session\. Setting this outside the block puts the session outside the firewall's coverage; \fBhdw4s firewall \-\-check\fR reports that\.
.TP
\fBHDW4S_ALLOW_SYSTEM_USER\fR
Set to \fByes\fR to permit a session for an account below UID 1000\.
.TP
\fBHDW4S_TRANSPORT\fR
\fBtcp\fR or \fBunix\fR\. Set with \fBhdw4s transport\fR, which also arranges the units that go with it, rather than by hand\.
.TP
\fBHDW4S_PROXY_GROUP\fR
The group allowed to open a session's Unix socket, i\.e\. the group the reverse proxy runs as\. Defaults to \fBwww\-data\fR\.
.TP
\fBHDW4S_AUTH\fR
\fBnone\fR or \fBbasic\fR\. Set with \fBhdw4s auth\fR, which also generates the secret\.
.TP
\fBHDW4S_SESSION\fR
The desktop to start; defaults to \fBgnome\-session\fR\. Anything that runs on X11 works, and nothing else in a session depends on the choice\. GNOME 50, which Ubuntu 26\.04 ships, removes the X11 session that the current Selkies release needs, whereas Xorg and the lighter desktops have no such plan, so changing this is the whole migration when that matters\.
.TP
\fBSELKIES_VERSION\fR
Pin a Selkies release and stop following upstream\.
.SH "SHARED HOME DIRECTORIES"
A GNOME session assumes it is the only one using its home directory\. That assumption breaks when the same home is mounted on more than one machine, or when another desktop is already logged in against it\. Two sessions then write the same settings database, the same keyring and the same metadata stores\. None of those are safe to share, and the settings database and the metadata stores use memory\-mapped and journalled files that are documented not to work over NFS at all\.
.P
Setting \fBHDW4S_ISOLATION=profile\fR keeps the shared home for the user's files but moves the session's settings, state and cache to a private directory on local disk\. Concretely, the session gets its own \fBXDG_DATA_HOME\fR, \fBXDG_STATE_HOME\fR, \fBXDG_CACHE_HOME\fR and dconf profile, its own X authority cookie, its own audio server socket, and wrappers that give Firefox and Thunderbird their own profiles\.
.P
\fBXDG_CONFIG_HOME\fR is deliberately left alone\. dconf does not support a per\-session config home, and some toolkits look for \fB~/\.config\fR regardless of it; the dconf profile is moved on its own instead, which is the supported way\.
.P
What stays shared:
.IP "" 4
.nf
Documents, Desktop, Downloads, Pictures, Music, Videos
everything else in the home directory, including ~/\.ssh
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
Existing settings can be carried across once with \fBhdw4s seed\fR, which is the only supported way to populate a profile from a home directory and does not run by itself\.
.P
What becomes per\-session:
.IP "" 4
.nf
desktop appearance, extensions, keyboard shortcuts
saved passwords and online accounts (a separate keyring; see `keyring`)
browser profile, history and open tabs
recently\-used files
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
One consequence is worth planning for: browsers download some data per profile, and an isolated session is a new profile\. A current Chrome fetches an on\-device language model of nearly three gigabytes that way, so a machine running several isolated sessions holds several copies of it on local disk and fetches each one over the network\. Where that matters, it can be turned off for every session at once with a browser policy, which is a decision for the administrator rather than something this package should make\.
.P
That split is not a compromise that can be tuned away\. Those components conflict precisely because they are shared, so the only way to stop the conflict is to stop sharing them\.
.SH "DISPLAY AND PORT ALLOCATION"
A session's TCP port comes from a slot recorded in \fB/etc/hdw4s/instances\fR\. The slot is assigned when the session is first enabled and never moves, so a reverse proxy and a firewall can both be configured against it and stay valid when accounts are added or removed\.
.P
The X display number is \fInot\fR allocated\. The X server picks the first free one itself and reports it back, which is the only way to claim one without a race\. Nothing outside the machine ever sees the display number, so it does not need to be predictable\.
.SH "TRANSPORTS"
How the reverse proxy reaches a session, in decreasing order of how much the machine itself can guarantee\.
.TP
\fBUnix socket\fR (\fBhdw4s transport\fR \fIinstance\fR \fBunix\fR)
The session listens only on loopback, and systemd exposes it at \fB/run/hdw4s\-proxy/<instance>\.sock\fR, owned by the group named in \fBHDW4S_PROXY_GROUP\fR\. Who may connect is then a question of file permissions: a process that cannot open the socket cannot reach the session at all, whatever address it comes from and whatever it claims to be\. There is no shared secret to leak and no address to spoof\.
.IP
This is the right answer whenever it is available\. It requires the proxy to be on the same machine, or to be able to see the same filesystem\.
.IP
Two containers on one host can share a socket if the same directory is bind\-mounted into both and they use the same user\-id mapping, which is the usual default\. It then behaves exactly as it would locally: file permissions decide who may connect, and \fBSO_PEERCRED\fR reports the peer's identity translated correctly into the reading side's namespace\. Containers with \fIdifferent\fR id mappings see different ownership on the same file, and that is where the arrangement stops working\.
.TP
\fBTCP with a proxy credential\fR (\fBtcp\fR plus \fBhdw4s auth\fR)
For a proxy on another machine\. The firewall restricts which addresses may connect, and the session additionally requires a secret that only the proxy knows, injected by it as a header\. An attacker who defeats the address check \-\- by spoofing, or simply by being on the same network \-\- still does not have the secret\.
.IP
The secret crosses the network in the clear on each request, so it is worth pairing with something that makes the path itself private, such as a point\-to\-point encrypted tunnel between the proxy and the session's host\.
.IP
It is also worth making the address check mean something\. Where the network can pin each host to the addresses it was assigned, a neighbour can no longer claim to be the proxy, which is otherwise the easy way past an address list\. Most hypervisors and many switches offer some form of this; consult their documentation, and check afterwards that a dynamically assigned address has not been left out\.
.TP
\fBTCP alone\fR (\fBtcp\fR, the default)
The firewall's address list is the only control\. Adequate on a network where nothing untrusted can route to the machine, and the weakest of the three otherwise\.
.SH "REVERSE PROXY AND SECURITY"
\fBA session performs no authentication of its own\.\fR It is built to sit behind a reverse proxy that has already identified the user, so that reaching the desktop takes no second login\. Anything that can open a TCP connection to a session port gets that user's desktop\.
.P
Two things follow, and both matter:
.IP "\[ci]" 4
\fBHDW4S_PROXIES\fR must list the proxy, and nothing else\. It is the only thing standing between a session and everything that can route to the machine\.
.IP "\[ci]" 4
The proxy must be the only path\. Terminate TLS there, authenticate there, and do not expose a session port by any other route\.
.IP "" 0
.P
The package owns one nftables table, \fBinet hdw4s\fR\. It restricts and does not grant\. A drop in this table overrides any other table's accept, so the port block can be closed reliably\. An accept in it is only advisory: if another firewall on the machine drops the packet first, nothing here brings it back, so opening the block to the proxy may need a rule wherever that firewall is configured\.
.P
The rules cover the whole port block rather than the ports currently in use\. A rule written per session goes stale the moment a session moves, which is how a firewall ends up guarding a port nothing listens on while live sessions sit unprotected beside it\.
.P
The table is also checked periodically, not just applied once at session start\. \fBnft flush ruleset\fR removes every table on the machine, including this one, and several common tools issue it \-\- \fBufw reload\fR and \fBnetfilter\-persistent\fR among them\. Without a check, the table would vanish while every session carried on serving, and nothing would say so\. \fBhdw4s\-firewall\.timer\fR notices and puts it back\.
.P
\fBhdw4s enable\fR refuses an account below UID 1000 unless \fBHDW4S_ALLOW_SYSTEM_USER=yes\fR is set, since a browser\-reachable desktop with no authentication of its own is rarely what is wanted for a system account\.
.P
Each session's X server is given its own authority cookie in its runtime directory\. Without one, every account on the machine could read the session's screen and type into it\.
.SH "FILES"
.TP
\fB/etc/hdw4s/hdw4s\.conf\fR
Defaults for every session\.
.TP
\fB/etc/hdw4s/<instance>\.conf\fR
Settings for one session\.
.TP
\fB/etc/hdw4s/instances\fR
Slot table mapping instances to ports\. Edit with \fBhdw4s release\fR, not by hand while a session is running\.
.TP
\fB/etc/hdw4s/<instance>\.keyring\.cred\fR
The session keyring's password, encrypted to this machine\. Created by \fBhdw4s keyring\fR, removed by \fBhdw4s release\fR\.
.TP
\fB/etc/hdw4s/nftables\.conf\fR
Generated ruleset\. Regenerated by \fBhdw4s firewall \-\-apply\fR\.
.TP
\fB/usr/lib/hdw4s/hdw4s\.xorg\.conf\fR
X server configuration for the \fBdummy\fR driver\. Deliberately not \fB/etc/X11/xorg\.conf\fR, which belongs to the machine's own display\.
.TP
\fB/var/lib/hdw4s/<instance>/\fR
An isolated session's profile\.
.TP
\fB/run/hdw4s/<instance>/\fR
Runtime directory: X authority cookie, X server log, current display\.
.SH "UPDATES"
A timer follows upstream daily\. It verifies what it downloaded before replacing anything, and refuses outright to install a release whose layout it does not recognise, rather than half\-upgrading a working machine\.
.P
It restarts only sessions nobody is connected to\. A session in use keeps the version it started with until it next stops on its own, which is when picking up a new one costs nothing\. An updater that reboots a desktop somebody is working in, at an hour chosen by a timer, is an updater that gets switched off\.
.SH "DIAGNOSTICS"
.nf
hdw4s list                       what exists and whether it is running
hdw4s show <instance>            effective settings and their source
hdw4s firewall \-\-check           whether every session is actually filtered
hdw4s \-\-version                  which version this is
systemctl status hdw4s@<i>       includes the display and port when running
journalctl \-xeu hdw4s@<i>        session output, including the X server
.fi
.P
The X server's log is kept in the session's runtime directory rather than the home directory, so it disappears with the session instead of accumulating\.
.SH "LIMITATIONS"
A second desktop for an account that is already logged in on the same machine works only because gnome\-session falls back to its own service manager when it cannot reach a systemd user manager, and there is only ever one of those per account\. GNOME 49 removes that fallback and GNOME 50 removes the X11 session altogether, so that arrangement does not survive an upgrade past Ubuntu 24\.04\.
.P
Sessions on \fIdifferent\fR machines sharing one home directory are not affected: the checks involved are local to a machine\.
.P
Neither limit reaches the rest of the design\. The X server, the streaming, the audio and the isolation are all indifferent to which desktop is started, so setting \fBHDW4S_SESSION\fR to one that is not being withdrawn from X11 \-\- Xfce, MATE, LXQt \-\- avoids both\. That is a smaller change than it sounds, and a much smaller one than bridging a Wayland session back onto an X11 display, which is possible but visibly a workaround\.
.P
Snap and Flatpak applications ignore the wrappers and some of the environment used to isolate a session, so they may still share state between desktops\.
